# Security

Security is a primary architectural concern.

Use defense in depth.

## Trust boundaries

Treat all external input as untrusted.

This includes:

- HTTP requests
- API requests
- route parameters
- query parameters
- headers
- cookies
- uploaded files
- imported GeoJSON
- external API responses
- client-side data
- background job payloads

## Authentication

Authentication must be server-side and explicit.

API authentication uses the approved JWT architecture.

Do not implement custom cryptography or token algorithms.

Use established, maintained libraries.

## Authorization

Authentication is not authorization.

Every protected operation must verify authorization.

Never rely solely on:

- hidden UI elements
- frontend permissions
- route obscurity
- client-side checks

Enforce authorization server-side.

## RBAC

Roles and permissions must be explicit. Using Spatie packages with admin configurable on UI.

Where district-level access applies, authorization must enforce the user's
assigned scope server-side.

Never assume that a user who can access one district can access another.

## Input validation

Validate input at trust boundaries.

Use allowlists where practical.

Do not rely on database errors as validation.

## Mass assignment

Use explicit, safe assignment of request data.

Never blindly map arbitrary request fields into persistent models.

## File uploads

Treat uploaded files as untrusted.

Validate:

- file type
- file size
- filename handling
- storage location
- access permissions
- content where appropriate

Never execute uploaded content.

## GeoJSON

Imported GeoJSON is untrusted input.

Validate:

- structure
- geometry type
- coordinate validity
- coordinate reference expectations
- size limits
- polygon validity where required

Do not trust client-provided geometry.

## Secrets

Never hardcode:

- passwords
- API keys
- JWT secrets
- private keys
- database credentials
- cloud credentials

Use environment-based or managed secret configuration.

Never output secrets in logs.

## Logging

Do not log:

- passwords
- authentication tokens
- otp codes
- session secrets
- private keys
- unnecessary sensitive personal data

## API security

Protect against:

- broken access control
- excessive data exposure
- injection
- replay where relevant
- brute force
- abuse
- insecure object references
- mass assignment
- improper error disclosure
- cors
- CSP
- bots

## Web security

Use appropriate:

- CSRF protection
- CSP
- secure headers
- cookie attributes
- HTTPS
- output escaping
- content-type handling

## SSRF

External URL fetching must be treated as a security-sensitive operation.

Never allow arbitrary internal-network access through user-controlled URLs.

## Error handling

Production errors must not expose:

- stack traces
- database credentials
- SQL
- filesystem paths
- internal service details
- secrets

## Security review

Significant security-sensitive changes require explicit security review.

Do not declare the system secure merely because tests pass.

## Investor verification

Investor email and phone verification are security trust boundaries.

Verification must be:

- server-side
- time-bound where tokens/OTP are used
- rate-limited where appropriate
- auditable
- resistant to replay
- protected against account enumeration where applicable

Do not expose verification tokens or OTP values in logs.

## Investor opportunity authorization

Approved/active opportunity access is an authorization boundary.

Do not assume that:

- registration grants opportunity access;
- authentication grants opportunity access;
- knowing an opportunity UUID grants opportunity access;
- hiding an opportunity in the UI protects it.

Protected opportunity endpoints must enforce authorization server-side.
